WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.100 --> 00:00:07.140
ABDUL KINADIYIL: Hello.

00:00:07.140 --> 00:00:10.570
I'm Abdul Haseen Kinadiyil,
Solution Architect at Google

00:00:10.570 --> 00:00:13.570
Cloud, specialized in Apigee.

00:00:13.570 --> 00:00:17.890
In this session, we are going to
discuss how API management play

00:00:17.890 --> 00:00:20.560
a critical role in your
application modernization

00:00:20.560 --> 00:00:21.980
journey.

00:00:21.980 --> 00:00:26.110
I'm joined by Chuck Rhoades
from Pizza Hut in this session.

00:00:26.110 --> 00:00:29.950
Chuck, can you talk a little bit
about your role at Pizza Hut?

00:00:29.950 --> 00:00:31.290
CHUCK RHOADES: Sure, Abdul.

00:00:31.290 --> 00:00:32.460
Hi, I'm Chuck Rhoades.

00:00:32.460 --> 00:00:36.360
I am the Director of E-Commerce
Technology for Pizza Hut US.

00:00:36.360 --> 00:00:39.420
So I've been doing a lot of work
on modernizing our platforms

00:00:39.420 --> 00:00:41.520
and in particular,
implementing Apigee

00:00:41.520 --> 00:00:43.390
as part of our tech strategy.

00:00:43.390 --> 00:00:44.940
So back to you, Abdul.

00:00:44.940 --> 00:00:46.860
ABDUL KINADIYIL: Thanks.

00:00:46.860 --> 00:00:49.090
This is the agenda
for today's session.

00:00:49.090 --> 00:00:51.750
First, we will see
the trends and pattern

00:00:51.750 --> 00:00:54.240
in application modernization.

00:00:54.240 --> 00:00:56.970
Moving to a microservices
architecture

00:00:56.970 --> 00:01:00.470
is essential in your
modernization journey.

00:01:00.470 --> 00:01:04.310
We will discuss what are the
key aspects of microservices.

00:01:04.310 --> 00:01:07.430
Then, Chuck will cover
the use cases and some

00:01:07.430 --> 00:01:09.620
of the challenges
the team encountered

00:01:09.620 --> 00:01:12.060
in the modernization effort.

00:01:12.060 --> 00:01:14.970
Then we'll take a look
at what API management

00:01:14.970 --> 00:01:18.180
and Apigee platform
brings to the table

00:01:18.180 --> 00:01:22.970
when it comes to modernizing
your applications.

00:01:22.970 --> 00:01:26.240
Today's customer needs
a seamless experience

00:01:26.240 --> 00:01:28.550
across devices.

00:01:28.550 --> 00:01:32.690
As a customer, I may start
an e-commerce transaction

00:01:32.690 --> 00:01:34.670
on my smartphone.

00:01:34.670 --> 00:01:37.850
And then later, I may
move to a wiser system,

00:01:37.850 --> 00:01:40.190
such as Google Home.

00:01:40.190 --> 00:01:43.700
Then I may complete that
order on my Google watch

00:01:43.700 --> 00:01:45.640
at the end of the day.

00:01:45.640 --> 00:01:48.600
The customer is expecting
seamless experience

00:01:48.600 --> 00:01:50.920
across these devices.

00:01:50.920 --> 00:01:54.330
The legacy applications that
we have in the enterprises

00:01:54.330 --> 00:01:57.960
are not meeting those demands.

00:01:57.960 --> 00:02:02.550
What are the requirements
for the modern applications?

00:02:02.550 --> 00:02:04.500
From the business
perspective, you

00:02:04.500 --> 00:02:07.370
want to increase revenue
from the business channels.

00:02:07.370 --> 00:02:10.490
And you also want to open
up new business channels.

00:02:10.490 --> 00:02:12.630
However, developers--
the developers

00:02:12.630 --> 00:02:17.760
want to build application
and deploy more frequently.

00:02:17.760 --> 00:02:21.870
We already saw the end users
want seamless experience

00:02:21.870 --> 00:02:23.040
across these devices.

00:02:25.990 --> 00:02:30.490
That brings us to the
composable enterprises.

00:02:30.490 --> 00:02:33.940
Every organization
out there is embracing

00:02:33.940 --> 00:02:36.040
digital transformation
and moving

00:02:36.040 --> 00:02:39.020
to a composable enterprise.

00:02:39.020 --> 00:02:43.070
That means today, you may have
a lot of monolith applications

00:02:43.070 --> 00:02:45.140
with business capabilities.

00:02:45.140 --> 00:02:47.780
You may want to expose
these business capabilities

00:02:47.780 --> 00:02:49.400
independently.

00:02:49.400 --> 00:02:52.250
And then, your
citizen developers,

00:02:52.250 --> 00:02:54.440
partners, third-party
developers will

00:02:54.440 --> 00:02:57.320
be able to leverage those
business capabilities

00:02:57.320 --> 00:03:00.240
and create new experience
to your end users.

00:03:02.880 --> 00:03:05.220
How do you modernize
your application?

00:03:05.220 --> 00:03:09.240
The answer is moving towards
a microservices architecture.

00:03:09.240 --> 00:03:13.020
Let's see what are the key
aspects of microservices.

00:03:13.020 --> 00:03:16.100
The first one is composability
and replaceability.

00:03:16.100 --> 00:03:18.800
That means you can take
these microservices,

00:03:18.800 --> 00:03:22.190
package them differently, and
give different experiences

00:03:22.190 --> 00:03:23.900
to our end users.

00:03:23.900 --> 00:03:27.860
You can replace one version of
the microservice with other.

00:03:27.860 --> 00:03:32.060
Technology heterogeneity--
that means you can develop

00:03:32.060 --> 00:03:34.250
microservices using
different technologies.

00:03:34.250 --> 00:03:38.640
One can be using Java and
other can be using Python.

00:03:38.640 --> 00:03:41.130
You can scale these
microservices independently

00:03:41.130 --> 00:03:42.970
of each other.

00:03:42.970 --> 00:03:45.810
And you are partitioning
that big task

00:03:45.810 --> 00:03:47.550
to smaller pieces,
when you move to

00:03:47.550 --> 00:03:49.950
a microservices architecture.

00:03:49.950 --> 00:03:53.340
You will have focused
teams dedicated

00:03:53.340 --> 00:03:56.250
to manage these
microservices that

00:03:56.250 --> 00:03:59.610
will allow you to move faster
and reduce the time to market.

00:04:02.260 --> 00:04:05.220
So when you are modernizing
your monolith application

00:04:05.220 --> 00:04:08.010
and moving to a
microservices architecture,

00:04:08.010 --> 00:04:11.070
this may be your future state
that you are envisioning

00:04:11.070 --> 00:04:13.320
for your application.

00:04:13.320 --> 00:04:16.620
You may have an e-commerce
monolith application

00:04:16.620 --> 00:04:19.980
with UI, then user
authentication, order

00:04:19.980 --> 00:04:23.220
management, inventory
lookup, et cetera.

00:04:23.220 --> 00:04:25.740
You want to take each
of those capabilities

00:04:25.740 --> 00:04:30.320
and move to independent
microservices.

00:04:30.320 --> 00:04:33.350
But the reality could
be somewhere in between,

00:04:33.350 --> 00:04:35.840
meaning you may still
have to leverage

00:04:35.840 --> 00:04:38.390
some of the capabilities
from the legacy monolith

00:04:38.390 --> 00:04:41.000
application.

00:04:41.000 --> 00:04:43.190
And some well-understood
capabilities

00:04:43.190 --> 00:04:46.100
such as inventory
management or order

00:04:46.100 --> 00:04:50.000
look up from the inventory--
from the e-commerce application

00:04:50.000 --> 00:04:53.330
will be moved to the
microservices architecture.

00:04:53.330 --> 00:04:55.660
Now, let me turn to Chuck.

00:04:55.660 --> 00:04:58.850
And let's hear from Chuck, how
did the modernization journey

00:04:58.850 --> 00:05:03.080
start for Pizza Hut, and what
are the challenges the team had

00:05:03.080 --> 00:05:05.420
in the early stages?

00:05:05.420 --> 00:05:06.950
CHUCK RHOADES: Thanks, Abdul.

00:05:06.950 --> 00:05:09.680
Much as Abdul has described,
where we started out

00:05:09.680 --> 00:05:12.170
on our modernization
journey was a mixture

00:05:12.170 --> 00:05:14.390
of a lot of the different
worlds that Abdul

00:05:14.390 --> 00:05:16.520
has described and talked about.

00:05:16.520 --> 00:05:18.710
In the center of our
e-commerce platform

00:05:18.710 --> 00:05:22.025
was an e-commerce core
monolith application

00:05:22.025 --> 00:05:25.340
that serviced all of the
major purposes and components

00:05:25.340 --> 00:05:27.320
of e-commerce
technology, as well

00:05:27.320 --> 00:05:31.040
as a lot of back-end reporting
capabilities, data ingestion,

00:05:31.040 --> 00:05:32.730
and things like that.

00:05:32.730 --> 00:05:34.940
In addition to that,
we had started creating

00:05:34.940 --> 00:05:36.800
several internal service APIs.

00:05:36.800 --> 00:05:40.400
Different components focused
on some parts of our technology

00:05:40.400 --> 00:05:43.220
like localizing customer
to the appropriate store

00:05:43.220 --> 00:05:46.760
for deliveries or carry-outs
and other things around Delivery

00:05:46.760 --> 00:05:48.410
Tracker and other
pieces that we built

00:05:48.410 --> 00:05:52.130
more recently in a more
modern, microservice-centric

00:05:52.130 --> 00:05:52.868
architecture.

00:05:52.868 --> 00:05:54.410
And then in addition
to that, we have

00:05:54.410 --> 00:05:57.890
things like our BI platforms,
store communication,

00:05:57.890 --> 00:06:00.600
back of house
systems, POSs, really

00:06:00.600 --> 00:06:04.160
a complicated and
wide-spanning technology stack,

00:06:04.160 --> 00:06:07.250
making up all of our
infrastructure for our commerce

00:06:07.250 --> 00:06:08.510
business.

00:06:08.510 --> 00:06:10.490
In the beginning,
where we started from,

00:06:10.490 --> 00:06:13.070
these technologies didn't
have a common way for us

00:06:13.070 --> 00:06:14.750
to use or access them.

00:06:14.750 --> 00:06:17.510
And so lots of connections
exist between all

00:06:17.510 --> 00:06:19.610
of the different
components and pieces.

00:06:19.610 --> 00:06:22.890
There many different ways of
accessing different components.

00:06:22.890 --> 00:06:25.790
Some of them are through
a modern idea of an API.

00:06:25.790 --> 00:06:29.030
Some are very older types of
technologies and approaches

00:06:29.030 --> 00:06:32.540
with TCP sockets or other
transport technologies,

00:06:32.540 --> 00:06:36.110
with no standards around how
we would access and utilize

00:06:36.110 --> 00:06:37.680
all of these services.

00:06:37.680 --> 00:06:39.980
So one of the first things
we really focused on

00:06:39.980 --> 00:06:45.000
was creating an API Facade
and security around our APIs.

00:06:45.000 --> 00:06:48.410
We did that by implementing
Apigee, which very quickly gave

00:06:48.410 --> 00:06:51.440
us the ability to establish a
common authentication structure

00:06:51.440 --> 00:06:54.680
and strategy with a common
security and authentication

00:06:54.680 --> 00:06:56.630
model, across all of
the things that we

00:06:56.630 --> 00:06:58.830
were converting into APIs.

00:06:58.830 --> 00:07:00.950
Along with that was
building out an API

00:07:00.950 --> 00:07:03.680
layer over the top of the
e-commerce core so that

00:07:03.680 --> 00:07:06.830
we could then plug all of the
functionality of that core

00:07:06.830 --> 00:07:10.460
into the new API gateway
layer that we had developed.

00:07:10.460 --> 00:07:12.440
This let us do two
things that were really

00:07:12.440 --> 00:07:15.260
important for promoting
the adoption of those APIs

00:07:15.260 --> 00:07:16.610
as we moved forward.

00:07:16.610 --> 00:07:19.670
One, we were able to use
Apigee's built-in developer

00:07:19.670 --> 00:07:22.940
portal technology to very
quickly and easily create a way

00:07:22.940 --> 00:07:25.790
to share the Swagger
documentation and the API

00:07:25.790 --> 00:07:28.040
schema spec
information around that

00:07:28.040 --> 00:07:30.920
with the consumers of
the API within Pizza Hut.

00:07:30.920 --> 00:07:33.100
So any future client
work that was being done

00:07:33.100 --> 00:07:36.380
could very quickly and easily
adopt and take advantage

00:07:36.380 --> 00:07:39.080
of those new APIs,
rather than going

00:07:39.080 --> 00:07:41.750
through the old ways of
integrating with the systems.

00:07:41.750 --> 00:07:45.110
Second, it allowed us to bring
a really good, clear point

00:07:45.110 --> 00:07:47.750
of view on what all of the
APIs and different technology

00:07:47.750 --> 00:07:50.780
points we had in the
system and start planning

00:07:50.780 --> 00:07:53.060
for how we could
modernize and bring

00:07:53.060 --> 00:07:57.800
those systems along the path of
becoming really future state,

00:07:57.800 --> 00:07:59.650
microservice-oriented
architecture.

00:07:59.650 --> 00:08:01.400
So with that, we want
to talk a little bit

00:08:01.400 --> 00:08:04.610
about what that modernization
journey can look like.

00:08:04.610 --> 00:08:07.010
There can be many paths
of getting from where you

00:08:07.010 --> 00:08:09.450
are today and over onto Cloud.

00:08:09.450 --> 00:08:11.780
As we illustrate in
this information here,

00:08:11.780 --> 00:08:13.200
you can start out on-prem.

00:08:13.200 --> 00:08:15.680
But sometimes, you start
out with lifted and shifted

00:08:15.680 --> 00:08:17.450
technologies sitting
in the Cloud,

00:08:17.450 --> 00:08:20.690
where you've taken a
legacy technology stack.

00:08:20.690 --> 00:08:23.000
You may have moved it
into a cloud provider,

00:08:23.000 --> 00:08:26.270
but you haven't really
modernized that technology.

00:08:26.270 --> 00:08:28.550
For Pizza Hut, we did a
little bit of all of these.

00:08:28.550 --> 00:08:30.920
We had some places
where we were very--

00:08:30.920 --> 00:08:33.260
we were in a position
to take a service

00:08:33.260 --> 00:08:35.150
and go ahead and
start from the ground

00:08:35.150 --> 00:08:37.370
up of breaking that down
in the microservices

00:08:37.370 --> 00:08:38.690
and carrying it forward.

00:08:38.690 --> 00:08:42.770
Other places we needed to shift
technology over into the Cloud,

00:08:42.770 --> 00:08:45.590
so that we could then begin
doing the breakdown there.

00:08:45.590 --> 00:08:47.060
One of the really
valuable things

00:08:47.060 --> 00:08:49.850
about having the API
management layer in place

00:08:49.850 --> 00:08:52.610
is it enables you to use all
of these different journeys

00:08:52.610 --> 00:08:55.097
or a mixture of them
at the same time.

00:08:55.097 --> 00:08:56.930
And so I'm going hand
it back over to Abdul,

00:08:56.930 --> 00:08:58.888
and he's going to talk
to you a little bit more

00:08:58.888 --> 00:09:00.800
about API management.

00:09:00.800 --> 00:09:02.120
ABDUL KINADIYIL: Thanks, Chuck.

00:09:02.120 --> 00:09:05.360
You may have heard from
Chuck that API management was

00:09:05.360 --> 00:09:09.020
a critical aspect in their
modernization effort.

00:09:09.020 --> 00:09:11.630
Apigee, the API
management platform,

00:09:11.630 --> 00:09:15.020
allows you to establish a
relationship between consumer

00:09:15.020 --> 00:09:18.950
developers and your
service producers,

00:09:18.950 --> 00:09:22.880
such as legacy application team
or cloud-native application

00:09:22.880 --> 00:09:24.900
team.

00:09:24.900 --> 00:09:27.750
Apigee will allow you to expose
your business capabilities

00:09:27.750 --> 00:09:33.360
as nice APIs, using the
features such as security,

00:09:33.360 --> 00:09:35.760
then bot detection,
mediation of protocol

00:09:35.760 --> 00:09:39.780
from SOAP to REST and
REST to SOAP, then

00:09:39.780 --> 00:09:42.570
operation management,
developer portal, et cetera.

00:09:45.160 --> 00:09:47.950
The developer portal
is a key feature

00:09:47.950 --> 00:09:50.140
within the Apigee platform.

00:09:50.140 --> 00:09:52.060
It will allow your
consumer developers

00:09:52.060 --> 00:09:56.950
to interact with the APIs,
see the catalog of APIs,

00:09:56.950 --> 00:09:59.260
register within the
developer portal,

00:09:59.260 --> 00:10:02.230
and start consuming the APIs.

00:10:02.230 --> 00:10:04.420
This gives a
first-class developer

00:10:04.420 --> 00:10:07.150
experience to your
consumer developers

00:10:07.150 --> 00:10:09.260
on the day one of your
modernization journey.

00:10:11.940 --> 00:10:15.300
Now let's see what are
the patterns when you are

00:10:15.300 --> 00:10:17.280
modernizing your application.

00:10:17.280 --> 00:10:20.120
The first one is
service modernization.

00:10:20.120 --> 00:10:23.190
In this pattern, you have
a legacy service running

00:10:23.190 --> 00:10:25.400
in your on-premise data center.

00:10:25.400 --> 00:10:29.120
And you are putting an
API facade on top of it

00:10:29.120 --> 00:10:32.840
and leveraging the capabilities
from that Apigee API management

00:10:32.840 --> 00:10:33.770
platform--

00:10:33.770 --> 00:10:38.920
such as API analytics, then bot
detection, protocol mediation,

00:10:38.920 --> 00:10:39.950
et cetera--

00:10:39.950 --> 00:10:42.890
and then exposing a nice
interface to your consumer

00:10:42.890 --> 00:10:44.450
developers.

00:10:44.450 --> 00:10:48.290
This gives a better experience
for end users and consumer

00:10:48.290 --> 00:10:50.560
developers.

00:10:50.560 --> 00:10:53.500
The next one is hybrid
application, in which

00:10:53.500 --> 00:10:55.210
you have the legacy service.

00:10:55.210 --> 00:10:58.220
You may run this legacy service
within your on-premise data

00:10:58.220 --> 00:11:01.120
center using a modern
infrastructure service,

00:11:01.120 --> 00:11:03.250
such as Kubernetes Engine.

00:11:03.250 --> 00:11:06.160
And then you may
combine that experience

00:11:06.160 --> 00:11:08.510
with some other
good technologies

00:11:08.510 --> 00:11:13.090
that Google Cloud offers, such
as data loss prevention, cloud

00:11:13.090 --> 00:11:15.820
storage, artificial
intelligence, and machine

00:11:15.820 --> 00:11:16.900
learning.

00:11:16.900 --> 00:11:19.240
This gives an
enriched experience

00:11:19.240 --> 00:11:22.220
to your consumer
developers and end users.

00:11:22.220 --> 00:11:24.130
They are not actually
aware of the fact

00:11:24.130 --> 00:11:26.767
that they are
interacting with a legacy

00:11:26.767 --> 00:11:27.850
service behind the scenes.

00:11:30.580 --> 00:11:33.700
The third pattern is
cloud-native application.

00:11:33.700 --> 00:11:35.920
You may have
Greenfield applications

00:11:35.920 --> 00:11:39.760
that you are developing using
cloud-native technologies.

00:11:39.760 --> 00:11:42.710
You can leverage cloud
infrastructure services,

00:11:42.710 --> 00:11:46.930
such as Google Kubernetes
Engine, Compute Engine.

00:11:46.930 --> 00:11:50.740
You can develop serverless
application using Cloud Run.

00:11:50.740 --> 00:11:53.560
And then you can leverage
the CI/CD capabilities

00:11:53.560 --> 00:11:55.690
using Cloud Build.

00:11:55.690 --> 00:11:59.390
And here you can still leverage
some of the cloud services,

00:11:59.390 --> 00:12:04.550
such as artificial intelligence,
Firestore, then cloud storage.

00:12:04.550 --> 00:12:07.540
So in this case,
your end consumer

00:12:07.540 --> 00:12:13.040
and consumer developers are
getting a modern experience.

00:12:13.040 --> 00:12:17.070
Some applications, you may
take these patterns as stages.

00:12:17.070 --> 00:12:20.330
The stage one, you
have a legacy service.

00:12:20.330 --> 00:12:22.220
And then you are
modernizing it, then

00:12:22.220 --> 00:12:25.550
moving to stage two, hybrid
application, and the stage

00:12:25.550 --> 00:12:28.200
three, cloud-native application.

00:12:28.200 --> 00:12:30.050
Other applications
you may directly

00:12:30.050 --> 00:12:33.890
jump to cloud-native
or hybrid application.

00:12:33.890 --> 00:12:35.940
That's perfectly fine.

00:12:35.940 --> 00:12:38.310
It is not one size fits all.

00:12:38.310 --> 00:12:42.020
You can actually take multiple
path to your destination.

00:12:42.020 --> 00:12:45.440
Now let's hear more
from Chuck on how

00:12:45.440 --> 00:12:47.900
did the team at
Pizza Hut decompose

00:12:47.900 --> 00:12:50.030
their monolith
application and move

00:12:50.030 --> 00:12:53.550
towards a microservices
architecture.

00:12:53.550 --> 00:12:54.920
CHUCK RHOADES: Thanks, Abdul.

00:12:54.920 --> 00:12:59.930
As we talked about before, once
we had an API gateway in place

00:12:59.930 --> 00:13:02.750
that we could use to bring all
of our connections and traffic

00:13:02.750 --> 00:13:04.850
into our platform,
that really enabled

00:13:04.850 --> 00:13:06.770
us to start looking
at how we wanted

00:13:06.770 --> 00:13:08.822
to break that
e-commerce core down

00:13:08.822 --> 00:13:11.030
and how we wanted to start
transforming a lot of that

00:13:11.030 --> 00:13:13.470
into microservice technology.

00:13:13.470 --> 00:13:16.280
It gave us a lot of
flexibility in doing so.

00:13:16.280 --> 00:13:18.620
Because once we had
established the facade for what

00:13:18.620 --> 00:13:21.590
that API was going to look
like through the gateway, how

00:13:21.590 --> 00:13:24.350
we enabled that with
the services behind it

00:13:24.350 --> 00:13:27.570
was much more flexible and had
a lot more room for change.

00:13:27.570 --> 00:13:30.680
So we started by taking small
pieces of that architecture

00:13:30.680 --> 00:13:33.740
that we could really make
independent and run separately.

00:13:33.740 --> 00:13:35.780
All the while, we could
keep the functionality

00:13:35.780 --> 00:13:38.540
in the monolithic core
that needed to stay there

00:13:38.540 --> 00:13:40.795
for a longer period of time.

00:13:40.795 --> 00:13:42.170
In addition to
this, this allowed

00:13:42.170 --> 00:13:45.170
us to tie these platforms
into the other services

00:13:45.170 --> 00:13:47.360
that we were running with
our internal service APIs

00:13:47.360 --> 00:13:50.720
and platforms and still be
able to control and manage

00:13:50.720 --> 00:13:54.845
all of the traffic going through
those APIs, as we went along.

00:13:54.845 --> 00:13:56.720
Now, one of the things
you may have learned--

00:13:56.720 --> 00:13:59.210
if you've already been through
a microservices migration

00:13:59.210 --> 00:14:03.080
yourself or some good tip
for you as you move into that

00:14:03.080 --> 00:14:04.250
world--

00:14:04.250 --> 00:14:08.450
is the visibility and management
becomes a really important

00:14:08.450 --> 00:14:11.510
component of what you're
doing with your microservices,

00:14:11.510 --> 00:14:14.960
as you move through
one of these journeys.

00:14:14.960 --> 00:14:18.500
With all of these new services
interacting and coming together

00:14:18.500 --> 00:14:21.380
to provide data and
capabilities to your customers,

00:14:21.380 --> 00:14:24.950
where that traffic goes and how
it flows through your system

00:14:24.950 --> 00:14:27.080
can become really complicated.

00:14:27.080 --> 00:14:29.840
Fortunately, we were able to
use a lot of the capabilities

00:14:29.840 --> 00:14:32.720
in Apigee along with some
of the SRE principles

00:14:32.720 --> 00:14:36.230
around golden signals to
capture the telemetry we needed

00:14:36.230 --> 00:14:38.480
and get that to our
logging infrastructure

00:14:38.480 --> 00:14:40.760
so that we can see
exactly what is going on

00:14:40.760 --> 00:14:42.560
from service to service.

00:14:42.560 --> 00:14:44.420
With that, using Apigee
and a combination

00:14:44.420 --> 00:14:46.190
of other technologies,
we're also

00:14:46.190 --> 00:14:50.120
able to manage that mesh in
how those services integrate

00:14:50.120 --> 00:14:52.450
and interact with one another.

00:14:52.450 --> 00:14:54.860
Now, Abdul is going to talk
you through a few more things

00:14:54.860 --> 00:14:57.560
about some of the capabilities
for service mesh management

00:14:57.560 --> 00:15:00.540
in Apigee, available today.

00:15:00.540 --> 00:15:02.430
ABDUL KINADIYIL: Thanks, Chuck.

00:15:02.430 --> 00:15:04.560
So you may have heard
from Chuck that when

00:15:04.560 --> 00:15:09.370
you have a lot of microservices,
that can become unmanageable.

00:15:09.370 --> 00:15:11.700
So Envoy is an open
source component

00:15:11.700 --> 00:15:16.030
that is popular in service
mesh and microservices world.

00:15:16.030 --> 00:15:18.570
It actually allows you
to dynamically route

00:15:18.570 --> 00:15:22.440
the requests, then
distribute it, tracing,

00:15:22.440 --> 00:15:25.250
and then observability
to your microservices.

00:15:25.250 --> 00:15:28.770
It also gives you support
for HTTP protocol,

00:15:28.770 --> 00:15:31.440
gRPC protocol, et cetera.

00:15:31.440 --> 00:15:34.140
Apigee has an Envoy adapter.

00:15:34.140 --> 00:15:38.670
And that gives you an ability
to enforce security policies

00:15:38.670 --> 00:15:41.220
across the board in a
more consistent manner,

00:15:41.220 --> 00:15:44.580
using app keys
and auth protocol.

00:15:44.580 --> 00:15:48.300
It will allow you to
throttle the microservices

00:15:48.300 --> 00:15:52.440
in a more consistent manner,
using distributed quota.

00:15:52.440 --> 00:15:57.330
You can collect analytics data
across microservices running

00:15:57.330 --> 00:15:59.190
in clusters and regions.

00:16:02.290 --> 00:16:05.200
In summary, let's
take a look at what

00:16:05.200 --> 00:16:07.810
are the some of the
challenges that we have

00:16:07.810 --> 00:16:10.540
seen across customers,
when they are modernizing

00:16:10.540 --> 00:16:12.020
the application?

00:16:12.020 --> 00:16:14.290
The first one is
business continuity.

00:16:14.290 --> 00:16:16.780
When you are modernizing
your application,

00:16:16.780 --> 00:16:19.120
your consumer
developers and end users

00:16:19.120 --> 00:16:21.010
should be able to interact
with your services

00:16:21.010 --> 00:16:23.310
without any disruption.

00:16:23.310 --> 00:16:25.560
Then, when you
move the workloads

00:16:25.560 --> 00:16:29.610
from your on-premise
data center to the Cloud,

00:16:29.610 --> 00:16:32.130
then the data can be
located everywhere.

00:16:32.130 --> 00:16:35.070
That means on-premise
data center, public cloud,

00:16:35.070 --> 00:16:36.285
or edge network.

00:16:36.285 --> 00:16:39.000
The security should not be
compromised when you are

00:16:39.000 --> 00:16:40.980
modernizing your application.

00:16:40.980 --> 00:16:42.600
Visibility is a key aspect.

00:16:42.600 --> 00:16:44.730
You should be able to know
how much of your traffic

00:16:44.730 --> 00:16:48.240
is going to the legacy services
within your on-premise data

00:16:48.240 --> 00:16:53.503
centers, versus the modern
microservices in Cloud.

00:16:53.503 --> 00:16:54.920
You should be able
to interoperate

00:16:54.920 --> 00:16:59.090
between the legacy service
and modern microservices.

00:16:59.090 --> 00:17:02.810
And then you should be able
to enforce security policies

00:17:02.810 --> 00:17:05.359
across microservices,
and you should

00:17:05.359 --> 00:17:10.800
be able to throttle those
services without any problem.

00:17:10.800 --> 00:17:12.780
How Apigee can help here?

00:17:12.780 --> 00:17:16.050
Apigee will allow you to
connect between the legacy

00:17:16.050 --> 00:17:18.150
service and microservices.

00:17:18.150 --> 00:17:20.970
It hides the
modernization complexity

00:17:20.970 --> 00:17:24.180
from your end users and
consumer developers.

00:17:24.180 --> 00:17:27.000
You can enforce
security policies

00:17:27.000 --> 00:17:30.490
more a consistent
manner, across the board.

00:17:30.490 --> 00:17:34.160
It will allow you to mediate
between cloud protocol, as well

00:17:34.160 --> 00:17:37.620
as enterprise security protocol.

00:17:37.620 --> 00:17:41.490
You can integrate with
existing IdP systems

00:17:41.490 --> 00:17:43.860
that you have in
your enterprise.

00:17:43.860 --> 00:17:45.480
Visibility is a key aspect.

00:17:45.480 --> 00:17:48.720
You can use Apigee
dashboard to view

00:17:48.720 --> 00:17:51.750
the traffic that is going
to your legacy services,

00:17:51.750 --> 00:17:54.070
versus microservices.

00:17:54.070 --> 00:17:57.670
Apigee will allow you to
put an API facade in front

00:17:57.670 --> 00:18:00.940
of the microservices, so
your consumer developers

00:18:00.940 --> 00:18:06.090
has a nice experience when
interacting with those APIs.

00:18:06.090 --> 00:18:08.520
You can manage
your microservices

00:18:08.520 --> 00:18:12.720
in a more consistent manner
by enforcing security policies

00:18:12.720 --> 00:18:16.370
across clusters and across
region using Apigee Envoy

00:18:16.370 --> 00:18:17.700
adaptor.

00:18:17.700 --> 00:18:20.280
You can also collect
analytics data

00:18:20.280 --> 00:18:23.230
and then view them in
a single pane of glass,

00:18:23.230 --> 00:18:26.750
which is Apigee dashboard.

00:18:26.750 --> 00:18:29.610
So where does Apigee
and API management

00:18:29.610 --> 00:18:32.330
fits in your technology
stack for modernizing

00:18:32.330 --> 00:18:33.950
your application?

00:18:33.950 --> 00:18:36.500
Google Anthos is
a great technology

00:18:36.500 --> 00:18:39.740
to use when you are
modernizing your application.

00:18:39.740 --> 00:18:42.020
You can leverage the
infrastructure service,

00:18:42.020 --> 00:18:44.750
such as Google
Kubernetes Engine.

00:18:44.750 --> 00:18:48.290
Then you can leverage the
dynamic routing capability,

00:18:48.290 --> 00:18:52.370
distributed tracing,
observability,

00:18:52.370 --> 00:18:54.570
using the service mesh.

00:18:54.570 --> 00:18:58.250
And you can also develop the
serverless technology app,

00:18:58.250 --> 00:19:00.350
using the Cloud Run.

00:19:00.350 --> 00:19:04.760
API management sits on top of
this entire technology stack.

00:19:04.760 --> 00:19:06.590
It allows you to
establish a relationship

00:19:06.590 --> 00:19:11.210
between your consumer developers
and service producers.

00:19:11.210 --> 00:19:14.870
You can build an ecosystem
of your consumer apps

00:19:14.870 --> 00:19:16.550
and developers.

00:19:16.550 --> 00:19:18.830
You can open up new
business channels,

00:19:18.830 --> 00:19:20.540
as you are exposing
your business

00:19:20.540 --> 00:19:23.910
capabilities as nice APIs.

00:19:23.910 --> 00:19:28.040
It gives you a platform
with security and operations

00:19:28.040 --> 00:19:32.150
capability to manage your
microservices across regions

00:19:32.150 --> 00:19:35.310
and across clusters.

00:19:35.310 --> 00:19:37.830
Before we wrap up,
let's take a look

00:19:37.830 --> 00:19:39.540
at what are the key
benefits that you

00:19:39.540 --> 00:19:42.930
get by leveraging API
management in your application

00:19:42.930 --> 00:19:44.670
modernization journey.

00:19:44.670 --> 00:19:47.220
The first one is, you will be
able to discover new business

00:19:47.220 --> 00:19:48.540
channels.

00:19:48.540 --> 00:19:52.530
You are exposing your business
capabilities as nice APIs.

00:19:52.530 --> 00:19:54.340
And then your
consumer developers,

00:19:54.340 --> 00:19:58.530
such as third-party developers,
partners, all your citizen

00:19:58.530 --> 00:20:01.590
developers, will be
able to use these APIs

00:20:01.590 --> 00:20:05.550
and create new experiences
for your end users.

00:20:05.550 --> 00:20:08.730
That will open up new
business channels for you.

00:20:08.730 --> 00:20:10.380
And then your
consumer developers

00:20:10.380 --> 00:20:13.620
are getting a first-class
developer experience,

00:20:13.620 --> 00:20:16.140
by coming to your
developer portal

00:20:16.140 --> 00:20:19.530
and interacting with
the APIs on the day one

00:20:19.530 --> 00:20:22.150
of your modernization journey.

00:20:22.150 --> 00:20:25.690
They are looking at your
APIs, interacting with them,

00:20:25.690 --> 00:20:29.540
and registering and
consuming those APIs.

00:20:29.540 --> 00:20:32.990
You can access the legacy and
modern services seamlessly

00:20:32.990 --> 00:20:34.670
using APIs.

00:20:34.670 --> 00:20:36.980
You can measure
the cloud adoption

00:20:36.980 --> 00:20:40.130
and how much of the traffic
is going to your legacy

00:20:40.130 --> 00:20:42.680
services versus the
Microsoft service

00:20:42.680 --> 00:20:44.940
that you are running
in the cloud.

00:20:44.940 --> 00:20:47.270
Now let's hear from
Chuck, what are

00:20:47.270 --> 00:20:49.670
the key benefits
the team experienced

00:20:49.670 --> 00:20:54.290
when they used API management
in their modernization journey?

00:20:54.290 --> 00:20:55.940
CHUCK RHOADES: Thanks, Abdul.

00:20:55.940 --> 00:20:57.900
All of the benefits that
Abdul has discussed,

00:20:57.900 --> 00:20:59.600
I could tell you
how Pizza Hut has

00:20:59.600 --> 00:21:01.540
experienced those firsthand.

00:21:01.540 --> 00:21:03.680
But key among those
is really our ability

00:21:03.680 --> 00:21:08.330
to accelerate adoption and
speed to market with our APIs.

00:21:08.330 --> 00:21:10.620
By having the API
facade in place,

00:21:10.620 --> 00:21:13.220
it enables us both to
launch new services,

00:21:13.220 --> 00:21:16.640
as well as migrate old
components into new services,

00:21:16.640 --> 00:21:19.430
without affecting the
downstream consumers.

00:21:19.430 --> 00:21:21.350
On top of that, having
the developer portal

00:21:21.350 --> 00:21:25.310
available to expose those APIs
to our consuming developers who

00:21:25.310 --> 00:21:28.460
can easily access the technology
and be interacting really

00:21:28.460 --> 00:21:31.220
quickly has enabled
us to deliver features

00:21:31.220 --> 00:21:33.680
into our mobile
platforms and websites

00:21:33.680 --> 00:21:36.720
with a greater speed
than we ever have before.

